$ grep -c "tests/conftest.py" sdist-contents.txt
0                                    <-- ABSENT. This is C-7A1-5.

# Why the sdist looked like it shipped the tests, but did not:
#
#   distutils' `sdist.add_defaults()` documents its default file list as
#   "README or README.txt / setup.py / tests/test*.py and test/test*.py / ...".
#
# The default is a *filename glob*, so it picks up `tests/test_*.py` and by
# construction excludes `tests/conftest.py` -- the one file the pytest harness
# needs. The defect was structural, not accidental: eight test modules shipped
# and the harness did not, so the sdist looked complete.
#
#   $ python3 -c "import inspect; from setuptools._distutils.command import \
#       sdist; print(inspect.getsource(sdist.sdist.add_defaults))"
#   def add_defaults(self) -> None:
#       """Add all the default files to self.filelist:
#         - README or README.txt
#         - setup.py
#         - tests/test*.py and test/test*.py
#         ...

# ===========================================================================
# GREEN -- MANIFEST.in with `recursive-include tests *.py`
# ===========================================================================

$ cat MANIFEST.in
include CMakeLists.txt
include LICENSE
include README.md
recursive-include src/kernels *.h *.hip
recursive-include src/python_bindings *.cpp
# The pytest harness must ship with the sdist: without tests/conftest.py the
# installed tree has no `matmul_module` fixture, so the GPU lane errors instead
# of skipping (G0 condition C-7A1-5). 7A-3 adds the fixture globs when its
# fixtures land.
recursive-include tests *.py

$ python setup.py sdist --dist-dir dist
